  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nVisible satellite imagery was showing clear skies across Southern \nCalifornia this morning. Easterly winds this morning peaked at 45 \nmph in the San Diego mountains. The combination of weak offshore \nflow and a building ridge of high pressure will keep high \ntemperatures today 10 to locally up to 20 degrees above average, \nwith the greatest departure from average along the coast. \n\nAn upper level ridge will continue to build over Southern California \nfor the first half of the week. This will maintain well above \naverage temperatures for coastal areas, even further inland in the \ndeserts high temperatures will remain up to 5 degrees above average. \nThe weak offshore flow is expected to continue through the middle of \nthe week as well although wind gusts are expected to be 35 mph or \nless. The building ridge and offshore flow will keep marine layer \nlow clouds and fog mostly absent from the coast through at least the \nmiddle of the week. \n\nGlobal ensembles have trended slower with the onset of precipitation \nlate in the week, with slight chances now beginning late Friday. \nSignificant differences remain in the evolution of the upper level \npattern as the jet tries to split. Around 60% of the ensemble \nmembers (as well as the deterministic EC and GFS) show a deep closed \nlow in the southern stream and digs it south off the coast of CA and \npotentially into northern Mexico Saturday. Models are notoriously \npoor at forecasting the track/timing of such systems, so if this \npattern holds the precipitation forecast will likely change - \npotentially significantly - over the next few days. Around 20% of \nthe members show a weaker open wave developing in the southern \nstream, which would be more progressive (precipitation starting and \nending earlier with less accumulation). The remaining 20% does not \nproduce any split flow with zonal winds across SoCal. Current NBM \nforecast favors the further south closed low scenario with higher \nprecipitation probabilities over San Diego County on Sunday (60-\n70%), decreasing to the north (45-60%). The only certain thing for \nFriday and Saturday is cooling with increasing cloud cover.",
